data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**A production-safe workflow:**\n\n1. **Confirm it’s a leak.** Track memory over time under comparable traffic, especially after garbage collection. Log `process.memoryUsage()` (`heapUsed`, `heapTotal`, `rss`, `external`, `arrayBuffers`) alongside request rate, workload, and GC activity. A rising RSS alone doesn’t prove a JS heap leak.\n2. **Find which memory is growing.** Rising `heapUsed` suggests retained JavaScript objects; rising `external`/`arrayBuffers` points toward Buffers or native resources; RSS growth without those may involve native allocations or allocator behavior.\n3. **Capture evidence carefully.** Reproduce on a canary or replica if possible. Take two heap snapshots—before and after sustained traffic—and compare **retained size**, dominators, and retaining paths. Allocation sampling can help pinpoint where objects are created. Snapshots can pause the process, need substantial extra memory/disk, and may contain secrets; don’t capture one on a critical instance without assessing the risk.\n4. **Check common causes.** Unbounded caches/maps, listeners or timers not removed, request data retained in globals/closures, unclosed sockets/streams, and growing queues.\n5. **Mitigate and verify.** Bound caches and queues, clean up resources, then confirm memory plateaus under the same workload. Rolling restarts can limit impact while investigating; raising the memory limit may buy time, but doesn’t fix a leak.\n\nStart with metrics and a canary snapshot rather than enabling heavy profiling across the fleet."}}

